<p>Iranian officials said indirect nuclear talks with the U.S. in Geneva made “good progress,” and that both sides agreed on a shared set of “guiding principles” that could anchor a future deal. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said major gaps remain, and that turning those principles into a detailed text will take time—so a deal is not close. The diplomacy is unfolding against a tense backdrop. President Trump has called for a quick agreement and warned of possible U.S. military action if talks collapse, while U.S. officials say Tehran still has not accepted all of the President’s “red lines”. Even so, officials on both sides said the talks would continue, keeping open a channel in a long-running dispute that each says it wants to resolve through negotiations.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Zp4wOAuGxlwBEmYtvrBAvKxMZ8pYT6uoKk2khZ5m22xdPI9L3_yyWrYUATuHkkIHVDcHsynqWCiUP7IFZA2uYvd7F_6oaEcwYXzi4m6i3Qs_4ENFLMrx1rMGWf6jANI4uWF4LD-yzqliJd57lXYUKieIlfgxFsY6WKuK_bWOaBmR9nbqW4D7Y49j9hS-6zP3Tq-PTv4oGqI-w_lT7Cs0-TrcEqP5sQJIOURhO1FHdtqn321CyKmdx38J35n2s7hbdth49h_vyAYzZI3hCdTZb6le561smk0O4JEKdtaAlAfELtbNh6iM2dLhBe7D_7K-edqVsOEnwyoodeAFaRNppM1rGCqO2td0Ifv6UQk5ik2ZFnkbyjclZ7D5DihljrwUKN_jJchgjBkNw3qeOyRH_yyMRrNTIIleA8_6CLPPeJKvgk75jE84LL9bIuxWl5mtRRwWR3rLJTc5PLhBcwtNSfUj4g' alt='Iran&#x27;s Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi in Geneva, Switzerland during the talks.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Diplomacy with pressure attached: Vice President JD Vance said Iran has not acknowledged all of President Trump’s “red lines.” The same day, Iranian rhetoric included a warning that Iran could sink a U.S. warship recently deployed to the region—an example of how negotiations are moving forward amid rising threats.</li><li>Sanctions relief remains central: Iran has long sought relief from sweeping U.S. sanctions, one of Tehran’s main incentives in the talks.</li><li>Oman as go-between: Oman mediated the Geneva round, with the U.S. and Iran speaking indirectly.</li><li>A business-minded pitch: Iran described an eventual deal as a “trillion-dollar opportunity” for American firms, pointing to access to its energy and mineral resources. Analysts called it a pitch designed for President Trump’s deal-making style.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi: Araghchi said the sides reached a preliminary understanding on “guiding principles” and are moving toward drafting a potential agreement. He also cautioned that closing the remaining gaps will take time, and that a deal is not imminent. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/17/us/politics/us-iran-nuclear-talks.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>U.S. Vice President JD Vance: Vance said Iran has not agreed to all of President Trump’s “red lines,” signaling that Washington still sees major requirements unmet after the Geneva round. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/iran-has-not-agreed-to-all-trumps-red-lines-jd-vance-after-geneva-talks-11051417#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li><li>Analysts cited in South China Morning Post: Analysts said Tehran’s “trillion-dollar” framing is meant to match President Trump’s deal-first instincts, spotlighting potential business gains alongside security terms. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/week-asia/politics/article/3343872/iran-tempts-trump-trillion-dollar-nuclear-deal-pitch'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Al-Monitor analysis: Al-Monitor said the path ahead looks fragile, with sharp warnings, hardened red lines, and increased military signaling complicating diplomacy even as talks continue. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/military-posturing-alongside-fragile-diplomacy-whats-next-us-iran-talks'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/17/iran-and-us-agree-on-guiding-principles-for-a-deal-say-iranian-officials_6750559_5.html'>Iran and US agree on &#x27;guiding principles&#x27; for a deal, say Iranian offi